# No Negative Assertions

State what a thing is, does, or should do. Do not clarify by listing what it is not.

Negative assertions introduce competing ideas into the reader's head, then ask them to discard those ideas. That muddies the subject instead of sharpening it.

## Forbidden Pattern

Do not write lists or chains of "not X, not Y, not Z" to narrow a subject.

Bad:
> Pick: make /payroll tell the truth. Not documents, not IHSS, not historical payroll, not due-date settings, not overtime-exemption persist.

Good:
> Pick: make /payroll tell the truth about current pay-period totals and approval state.

## Rules

- Lead with the affirmative claim: what exists, what changes, what the goal is.
- If scope must be narrowed, name the included scope. Do not name the excluded neighbors.
- Exception: a single contrast is fine when it resolves a real ambiguity the reader already has (e.g. "update the serializer, not the model"). Do not stack multiple exclusions.
- Applies to chat replies, commit messages, plan docs, and any other agent-authored prose.
